# main.crude: the smallest project that runs.
#
# Everything a crude program can do without a window, a device or a patch is
# here: LOG prints to the terminal and files what the run's log level keeps in
# the project's own log (logs/hello-terminal.log), MODE() and FPS() describe the
# run this manifest asked for, TIME() is the clock the frame loop paces itself
# against, and STR()/NUM() turn values into words.
#
# Run it:  python -m crude run examples/hello_terminal
# Check it: python -m crude check examples/hello_terminal

LOG("hello from a crude project")
LOG("this run is " + MODE() + " at " + STR(FPS()) + " fps")

# A counting loop. VAR inside a WHILE body updates the variable the loop tests,
# so this needs no separate counter and cannot run away.
VAR line = 0
WHILE line < 3 THEN
    VAR line = line + 1
    LOG(STR(line) + ": the script owns its own output")
END

# RES reaches the files the manifest declares, and EXIT answers a code the shell
# can test. This project declares no files, so both are only mentioned.
#
#   LOG(GET(RES("readme"), "path"))
#   EXIT(0)

# LOG's second argument is the level. A debug line is not said out loud, so the
# terminal stays clean while the detail is still on disk - and how much of the
# run the file keeps is the run's own choice, since a project log defaults to
# warnings and errors:
#
#   python -m crude run examples/hello_terminal --log info
#   python -m crude run examples/hello_terminal --log debug
LOG("the loop above counted three times", "debug")

LOG("the run has taken " + STR(TIME()) + " second(s)")
